 ## Integration and repair
 
-In parallel mode, each accepted writer is integrated as soon as it is ready (`git merge --no-ff` into your checkout, one at a time). A task that depends on a writer starts only after that writer is integrated, so its new worktree contains the prerequisite. Serial mode needs no integration: the writers already worked in your checkout. A failed merge blocks the run and keeps every worktree and branch. The report lists each one with its cleanup command. A review repair creates a `repair-N` task: in parallel mode it gets a new worktree from the integrated HEAD; in serial mode it works in your checkout.
+In parallel mode, each accepted writer is integrated as soon as it is ready (`git merge --no-ff` into your checkout, one at a time). A task that depends on a writer starts only after that writer is integrated, so its new worktree contains the prerequisite. Serial mode needs no integration: the writers already worked in your checkout. A failed merge blocks the run and keeps every worktree and branch. The report lists each one with its cleanup command. A worktree the run can no longer prove is its own (recorded by an older version, or its `.git` file rewritten since) is never removed: the report lists it and its branch under Unproven resources for you to check. A review repair creates a `repair-N` task: in parallel mode it gets a new worktree from the integrated HEAD; in serial mode it works in your checkout.
 
